# Global rules (must follow)

## Stack & architecture
- Use Next.js App Router + TypeScript + React.
- Use Firebase Auth (email/password + Google) and Firestore.
- NEVER access Firestore directly from React components.
- All Firestore reads/writes must go through `src/services/*`.
- React components can only consume data through hooks in `src/hooks/*`.

## Folder structure (required)
- `src/lib/firebase.ts` initializes Firebase client SDK.
- `src/lib/env.ts` validates required env vars and throws clear errors if missing.
- `src/types/*` contains domain types (no duplication of shapes across files).
- `src/services/*` contains data access functions only (no UI).
- `src/hooks/*` contains hooks (auth + data).
- `src/components/*` contains reusable UI.

## Coding standards
- TypeScript strict: avoid `any`.
- Always handle loading, error, and empty states in UI.
- Keep functions small and single-responsibility.
- Prefer explicit names over abbreviations.

## Security non-negotiables
- Client must never be able to set `users.role` or `payments.status`.
- Admin role cannot be self-assigned from the client.
- If a feature affects auth/roles/payments, require a short plan + test checklist before coding.
